No. On Volta or newer, `__activemask()` is a snapshot of the lanes executing that instruction—not a guarantee that all 19 lanes in the branch are present. Starting converged does not guarantee they remain converged inside the branch, so a caller’s mask may omit physical lane 18.

For `__shfl_sync`, each caller must have its own bit set in the mask, and every non-exited lane named in that mask must execute the same shuffle intrinsic with the same mask. The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle; otherwise the retrieved value is undefined. With width 32 and source 18, the source is physical lane 18. Width does not add participants, and synchronization does not repair an incomplete mask.

Construct the membership mask with a ballot before diverging:

```cpp
__device__ float read_lane18(float value) {
    unsigned linear = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    unsigned lane = linear & 31u;
    unsigned participants = __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, lane <= 18u);

    if (lane <= 18u) {
        value = __shfl_sync(participants, value, 18, 32);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Call this function unconditionally from all 32 lanes of each full physical warp, with no prior lane exits. The ballot produces `0x0007ffff`; exactly lanes 0 through 18 call the shuffle with that mask, so all 19 receive lane 18’s input float. The other 13 lanes never call the shuffle and return their own input.

Use five reduction shuffles followed by one broadcast: exactly six float shuffle calls per caller. The fixed mask names only physical lanes 0 through 18, so the other thirteen lanes never need to call this function.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Preconditions: physical lanes 0..18 of a warp each call once;
// physical lanes 19..31 do not call. Each caller supplies its float.
__device__ float sum19(float value) {
    const unsigned linear = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linear & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    #pragma unroll
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const unsigned partner = lane + static_cast<unsigned>(offset);
        const bool has_partner = partner < 19u;
        const int source = static_cast<int>(has_partner ? partner : lane);
        const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, value, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            value = value + other;
        }
    }
    return __shfl_sync(mask, value, 0, 32);
}
```

Compile this device code with NVCC for `sm_70` or newer and call it from a CUDA kernel under the stated participation preconditions; no standalone host launcher is included.

The addition order is explicitly offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, then 1. At each stage, the shuffle reads the partner’s value from before that stage’s addition, and a lane without a partner leaves its value unchanged.

To prove exact contribution, initially lane ℓ holds only input ℓ. After processing offset d, it holds inputs at indices ℓ + kd below 19, for integers k from 0 through 32/d − 1. Inductively, the previous value contains the even-k indices, and the partner’s previous value contains the odd-k indices: these sets are disjoint and together give precisely the stated set. If the partner is out of range, the odd-k set is empty. Thus, after offset 1, lane 0 contains each of the nineteen inputs exactly once, subject to floating-point rounding; the final shuffle broadcasts that value to every caller.

Every shuffle source is either a valid partner in lanes 0 through 18 or the caller itself; the final source is lane 0. Every named lane executes all five reduction collectives and the broadcast with the same mask, including lanes without partners—the conditional skips only the addition, never the shuffle. The `_sync` collectives provide the required rendezvous, so this does not assume implicit lockstep execution.

Use circular partial sums: each lane builds a 16-input sum, then adds a three-input sum covering the remaining inputs. This uses exactly six float shuffles per caller and finishes with an addition—not a broadcast of any lane’s completed sum.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Preconditions: physical lanes 0..18 each call once;
// lanes 19..31 do not call. All callers belong to one warp.
__device__ float sum19(float value) {
    const unsigned linear = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linear & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    const float next = __shfl_sync(
        mask, value, static_cast<int>((lane + 1u) % 19u), 32);
    const float two = __fadd_rn(value, next);

    const float third = __shfl_sync(
        mask, value, static_cast<int>((lane + 2u) % 19u), 32);
    const float three = __fadd_rn(two, third);

    float partial = two;
    #pragma unroll
    for (unsigned offset = 2u; offset <= 8u; offset <<= 1u) {
        const float other = __shfl_sync(
            mask, partial,
            static_cast<int>((lane + offset) % 19u), 32);
        partial = __fadd_rn(partial, other);
    }

    const float tail = __shfl_sync(
        mask, three, static_cast<int>((lane + 16u) % 19u), 32);
    return __fadd_rn(partial, tail);
}
```

Compile as a CUDA translation unit with `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c sum19.cu`, or target a newer architecture. Call only from lanes 0–18 of a full physical warp, with all nineteen reaching this function once.

Addition order: interpreting indices modulo 19, define `S1(i) = input[i]` and `S2(i) = RN(S1(i) + S1(i+1))`. The code separately forms `S3(i) = RN(S2(i) + S1(i+2))`, then forms `S4`, `S8`, and `S16` using `S2k(i) = RN(Sk(i) + Sk(i+k))`. Each caller returns `RN(S16(i) + S3(i+16))`; `__fadd_rn` specifies round-to-nearest additions.

Exact-once coverage follows by induction: each doubling combines two disjoint consecutive intervals, so `S16(i)` contains offsets 0–15 exactly once. Its final partner contains offsets 16–18 exactly once. Together, these nineteen consecutive offsets modulo 19 cover every input exactly once, although rounding—and potentially overflow—can affect the numerical result.

Every source index is reduced modulo 19, so every shuffle reads a participating lane. All nineteen callers execute the same six collectives, in the same order, with the same mask and width; each caller’s own bit is set. There are no partner-dependent skips, and the synchronized shuffles provide the required rendezvous without assuming implicit lockstep.

Interpreting “Root” as “route”: use Bellman–Ford on only the vertices reachable from s that can also reach t. A negative cycle makes this query unbounded exactly when s can reach that cycle and the cycle can reach t.

```python
from typing import Iterable, Optional, Tuple


def classify_root(
    N: int,
    edges: Iterable[Tuple[int, int, int]],
    s: int,
    t: int,
) -> Tuple[str, Optional[int]]:
    edges = list(edges)
    forward = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        forward[u].append(v)
        reverse[v].append(u)

    def reachable(start, graph):
        seen = [False] * N
        seen[start] = True
        stack = [start]
        while stack:
            u = stack.pop()
            for v in graph[u]:
                if not seen[v]:
                    seen[v] = True
                    stack.append(v)
        return seen

    from_s = reachable(s, forward)
    if not from_s[t]:
        return ("unreachable", None)

    to_t = reachable(t, reverse)
    relevant = [from_s[v] and to_t[v] for v in range(N)]
    k = sum(relevant)
    active_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if relevant[u] and relevant[v]
    ]

    dist = [None] * N
    dist[s] = 0

    for _ in range(k - 1):
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in active_edges:
            if dist[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
                dist[v] = candidate
                changed = True
        if not changed:
            return ("finite", dist[t])

    for u, v, w in active_edges:
        if dist[u] is not None:
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
                return ("unbounded", None)

    return ("finite", dist[t])
```

Use Python 3; edges are triples `(u, v, weight)`, with valid zero-based vertices. For example, `classify_root(2, [(0, 1, -5)], 0, 1)` returns `("finite", -5)`. Python integers avoid overflow.

**Invariant and correctness.** Every finite distance is the cost of an actual walk from s. After i complete relaxation passes, each distance is no greater than the cost of any walk to that vertex using at most i edges; in-place updates may additionally discover longer walks.

Every vertex on an s-to-t walk is relevant, so filtering cannot remove a candidate answer. If the relevant graph has no negative cycle, deleting cycles from a walk never increases its cost, so a minimum-cost simple path exists with at most k−1 edges. Consequently, after k−1 passes the distances are exact, and no edge can relax.

Conversely, all relevant vertices have finite distances after those passes. If no edge could relax, every edge would satisfy `dist[v] <= dist[u] + w`; summing these inequalities around a negative cycle would imply `0 <=` a negative number, a contradiction. Thus an additional relaxable edge detects a relevant negative cycle. Repeating that cycle before continuing to t gives arbitrarily negative costs. Early termination is also safe: a pass with no changes leaves every edge inequality satisfied.

For s equal to t, the empty walk has cost zero; the answer is unbounded only if a reachable negative cycle can return to s. Parallel edges and self-loops require no special handling.

**Worst-case bounds.** With M edges, time is O(N + M + NM), and auxiliary memory is O(N + M). These are arithmetic-operation bounds; stored distances have O(log(NM·W + 1)) bits for maximum absolute weight W, so arbitrary-precision arithmetic adds the corresponding bit-cost factor.

Both reachability conditions matter: a negative cycle makes the query unbounded exactly when it is reachable from s and can reach t. The implementation below uses deterministic Bellman–Ford, not a queue heuristic, and preserves the identifier classify_root.

```python
from typing import Iterable, Optional, Tuple


def classify_root(
    N: int,
    edges: Iterable[Tuple[int, int, int]],
    s: int,
    t: int,
) -> Tuple[str, Optional[int]]:
    edges = list(edges)
    forward = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        forward[u].append(v)
        reverse[v].append(u)

    def reachable(start, graph):
        seen = [False] * N
        seen[start] = True
        stack = [start]
        while stack:
            u = stack.pop()
            for v in graph[u]:
                if not seen[v]:
                    seen[v] = True
                    stack.append(v)
        return seen

    from_s = reachable(s, forward)
    if not from_s[t]:
        return ("unreachable", None)

    to_t = reachable(t, reverse)
    relevant = [from_s[v] and to_t[v] for v in range(N)]
    k = sum(relevant)
    active_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if relevant[u] and relevant[v]
    ]

    dist = [None] * N
    dist[s] = 0

    for _ in range(k - 1):
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in active_edges:
            if dist[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
                dist[v] = candidate
                changed = True
        if not changed:
            return ("finite", dist[t])

    # Detection scan: do not modify distances.
    for u, v, w in active_edges:
        if dist[u] is None:
            continue
        candidate = dist[u] + w
        if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
            return ("unbounded", None)

    return ("finite", dist[t])
