Usage: run under Python 3 and call classify_root(N, edges, s, t), where each edge is a tuple (u, v, weight) and all vertex identifiers are valid zero-based indices. No external packages are required.

Invariant: every non-None distance is the cost of an actual walk from s in the relevant subgraph. After i complete passes, each distance is no greater than the cost of any walk to that vertex using at most i edges. Updates are in-place, so a pass can propagate farther; distances do not necessarily represent only walks of at most i edges. The guard `if dist[u] is None: continue` prevents an unreachable distance from entering arithmetic.

Unboundedness is established by a strictly improving edge in the detection scan after k−1 passes. Every relevant vertex is reachable within the relevant subgraph, so its distance is then finite. If there were no negative cycle there, removing nonnegative cycles would give a minimum-cost simple path with at most k−1 edges; the invariant would therefore make every distance optimal, ruling out any improvement. Conversely, if a negative cycle exists, having no improving edge would imply `dist[v] <= dist[u] + w` around the whole cycle; summing these inequalities contradicts its negative total weight. Such a cycle can be reached from s and followed by a route to t, so repeating it produces arbitrarily negative costs.

An unchanged pass permits early return because every relevant vertex has a finite distance and all edge inequalities hold; these inequalities bound every walk’s cost below by its destination distance, which is itself realized by a walk.

When s equals t, the empty walk makes the target reachable. The answer is finite with cost 0 unless a negative cycle is reachable from s and can return to s. A negative self-loop is detected even when k equals 1 and there are no ordinary relaxation passes.

Let M be the number of edges and m the number of active edges. Reachability and filtering take O(N+M) time; relaxation takes O(km), giving deterministic worst-case O(N+M+NM) time and O(N+M) memory for every allowed input. Parallel edges and self-loops require no special handling. Python integers avoid overflow; their bit lengths remain bounded by O(log(1+NM·10^12)), so accounting explicitly for integer arithmetic multiplies the relaxation bound by that bit-length factor.

The violated invariant is that every thread in the block must reach the same block-wide barrier. With 96 threads and valid = 70, threads 0–69 execute `__syncthreads()` while threads 70–95 skip it. That is invalid synchronization and can explain the stalled worker, but it does not guarantee a hang on every device; successful immediate launch checks do not establish successful kernel completion.

